<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
    wis_config.xml.template — production RTI Web Integration Service config for the
    Phase 16 v1 mesh dashboard (docs/cpp_router/implementation-plan.md#phase-16, design:
    docs/cpp_router/gui-visualization.md).

    LAN-side, not WAN (scope correction, 2026-07-21): subscribes to ActRouterMeshStatus on
    the C2/control node's admin LAN participant (control_lan, domain 20 in
    router/config/control-platform.yaml), not RouterHealth on the WAN. Reasons this
    replaced the original WAN-based plan:
      1. User direction: WIS should read the LAN-side "full resolution" router topic, not
         the WAN presence heartbeat.
      2. Independently confirmed while building the WAN version: control_wan/platform_wan
         use wan_qos_lib.xml profiles with discovery.initial_peers set to an explicit unicast
         peer list and multicast_receive_addresses disabled ($(WAN_RECEIVE_MULTICAST)) — an
         arbitrary external subscriber like WIS isn't in that peer list, so it can't
         passively discover WAN participants the way the original doc assumed. The LAN
         admin participant carries no such restriction.
      3. ActRouterMeshStatus is a strictly richer dataset for this purpose anyway: each
         peer entry is that peer's COMPLETE last RouterHealth summary (RouterMeshPeer.health)
         plus last_seen_delta_ms, not just the trimmed peers_seen refs RouterHealth itself
         carries — "full resolution", per the user's own framing.

    Trade-off this accepts (see gui-visualization.md): control_lan is node-local, so this
    WIS instance only sees the mesh from the C2/control node's own point of view. That
    node's RouterMeshStatus already aggregates every peer it knows about (D75/D76 presence
    design), so this is not "only this node" — it's "the whole mesh, as seen by this node" —
    but it is a single vantage point, not the WAN-wide, run-anywhere model Option A
    originally proposed.

    The placeholder line below (a single all-caps token, substituted verbatim by
    generate_wis_config.sh's splice step) is replaced at deploy time with the types block
    produced by:
        rtiddsgen -convertToXml -d <tmp> router/admin/RouterAdminTypes.idl
    RouterAdminTypes.idl stays the one source of truth; this XML is a regenerated build
    artifact, never hand-maintained.

    Custom reader QoS IS required here (unlike the retired WAN version) — a real bug found
    and fixed while testing this config against a live mesh, 2026-07-21: PresenceMonitor's
    mesh_writer_qos (router/src/core/PresenceMonitor.cxx) writes ActRouterMeshStatus as
    RELIABLE + TRANSIENT_LOCAL + KEEP_LAST(1), and the publish is CHANGE-DRIVEN ONLY (no
    periodic republish — "republished on roster change", see the IDL comment on
    RouterMeshStatus) — unlike RouterHealth's 1 Hz heartbeat, which papers over this same
    gap by writing a fresh sample every second regardless. WIS's DEFAULT reader QoS is
    VOLATILE, and a VOLATILE reader does NOT receive a TRANSIENT_LOCAL writer's
    already-written history on match (confirmed via ask_connext_question + observed
    directly: SEDP discovery completed — control_lan's log shows `subscription_discovered
    topic=ActRouterMeshStatus` for WIS's own GUID — but the reader still returned zero
    samples indefinitely, because the roster hadn't changed again since WIS matched, and a
    VOLATILE reader only gets samples written AFTER it matches, never the writer's stored
    late-joiner history). Fix: give the data_reader explicit TRANSIENT_LOCAL durability
    (matching the writer) via the qos_library/profile below, so the roster's current state
    replays on match instead of requiring a fresh roster change to ever see anything.

    register_type/topic naming: unlike the retired WAN version (where the topic name
    "RouterHealth" collided with the struct name and needed a distinct registration alias,
    RTIXMLObject_addChild error, spike finding 2026-07-21), the topic name here
    ("ActRouterMeshStatus") already differs from the struct name ("RouterMeshStatus"), so no
    alias is needed — register_type's name is exactly "RouterMeshStatus", matching the
    generated type verbatim (a real WAN-version bug found and fixed here: giving
    register_type an alias that differs from the real struct name broke SEDP type-name
    matching entirely — "type names ... do not match" — the reader must advertise the exact
    same type name the writer does, not just a type_ref pointing at it).
-->
<dds xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="__NDDSHOME__/resource/schema/rti_web_integration_service.xsd">

    __ROUTER_ADMIN_TYPES__

    <qos_library name="MeshDashboardQosLib">
        <qos_profile name="MeshStatusReaderQos">
            <datareader_qos>
                <durability>
                    <kind>TRANSIENT_LOCAL_DURABILITY_QOS</kind>
                </durability>
                <reliability>
                    <kind>RELIABLE_RELIABILITY_QOS</kind>
                </reliability>
            </datareader_qos>
        </qos_profile>
    </qos_library>

    <domain_library name="RouterAdminDomainLibrary">
        <domain name="ControlLanDomain" domain_id="20">
            <register_type name="RouterMeshStatus" type_ref="RouterMeshStatus" />
            <topic name="ActRouterMeshStatus" register_type_ref="RouterMeshStatus" />
        </domain>
    </domain_library>

    <web_integration_service name="mesh_dashboard">
        <application name="MeshDashboardApp">
            <domain_participant name="MeshParticipant"
                    domain_ref="RouterAdminDomainLibrary::ControlLanDomain">
                <subscriber name="MeshSubscriber">
                    <data_reader name="MeshStatusReader" topic_ref="ActRouterMeshStatus">
                        <datareader_qos base_name="MeshDashboardQosLib::MeshStatusReaderQos" />
                    </data_reader>
                </subscriber>
            </domain_participant>
        </application>
    </web_integration_service>
</dds>
